Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. Dispute 21 - Day 9:
5 Ways to Defeat Debt Collectors

Way No.4 - Best Practices for Dealing with Debt Collectors

In the last lesson we discussed the illegitimate practices of Collection
Agencies used to trick consumers into paying debts before having them
validated. Many of those practices are forms of harassment and
violations that, as a credit repair specialist, you should be aware of
so that you're instantly able to recognize them, point them out and
use them as grounds for deletion. 

In today's lesson, we're going to take a look at ways to respond to
these unlawful practices and the proper procedures to follow when
dealing with debt collectors and credit card debt. 

Important: Please understand that when following these practices, you
are performing these tasks and preparing these letters 

**for** the client. Unless you use a strategy of acting on behalf of the
client, you should not address your dispute letters or validation
requests as if they are coming from you (a repair company) on behalf of
the client. The letters should look/look like the client has written the
letters themselves.

Now Bruce, let's jump into it...

1)     Always deny and dispute and never admit that a debt is yours
or belongs to your client. It's not a "lie," or a reflection on your
honesty or character; This is a legal strategy. Remember, credit card
debt is unsecured. Credit Card Holders do not sign a contract.

2)     Despite the FDCPA's reference to responding to a
mini-Miranda within 30 days, 

**it is never too late to ask for validation**. Late is better than
never. Validation is key to your success.

3)     Always send a deny/dispute/debt validation letter in
response to 

**each** new mini-Miranda notice from new collectors.

4)     When your client tells you that they've received a call or
any form of contact from a new collection agency or junk debt buyer that
you've never heard of before, always take a few minutes to Google them
for more information and search the debt forums for comments about
others' experience with them.

5)     Understand that while the debt may belong to the client, it
is their legal right to deny it, to dispute it and to insist that the
collection agency or attorney document their case against your client,
proving - 
(1) That there was a contract between the client and the original
creditor, 
(2) That they can document their ownership of the debt if they are not
the original creditor, and
(3) How they arrived at the specific amount of the debt they say the
client owes.

6)     Unlike settling a debt with the original creditors or credit
card banks, you educate the client properly to try their best never to
settle with collection agencies and junk debt buyers. It may hurt the
credit even more. If the client chooses to settle, when figuring a
dollar amount, always remember that a Junk Debt Buyer bought the debt
for a few pennies on the dollar. You will also want to make sure that
the payment is reasonable enough to be paid in one lump sum and
negotiating a pay for delete. The way the debt collection industry is
set up, it will do a disproportionate amount of damage to the client
trying to repay an old debt. Making payments on older debts restarts the

**statute of limitations**, which goes from the date of the last payment
made on a debt. So, if the statute of limitations is three years, and
your client starts paying in installments after two years, the junk debt
buyer or collection agency can get a judgment against the client for
another three years instead of another year. In contrast and in relation
to the 

**7 ½ year reporting period**, that is not true for accounts reporting
on the credit report. Whether you pay the junk debt buyer or not, the
listing must come off your report within 7 years plus 180 days of the
date of first delinquency that led to the charge-off with the original
creditor.

7)     Never contact a debt collector for your client or advise your
client to contact a debt collector. Stay off the phone. Do not try to
handle things solely by phone. There is no record of a phone call and no
reason why the collecting party should hold up their end of the deal. Do
not talk to debt collectors on the phone unless you are attempting to
negotiate a settlement that will be followed up in writing. Even then,
you can initially propose a settlement through writing, expecting to get
something back in writing from the collector so that you have
documentation of their acceptance of the settlement offer in case
something were to happen and they tried to sale the account or continue
collecting after the agreed settlement amount was paid. 

8)     Advise your client never to take telephone calls from debt
collectors or return their calls as a result of threatening telephone
messages. If they have sent a mini-Miranda and you have responded
properly to it in writing, you have instructed them not to call and by
doing so they are in violation of the FDCPA. Remember...If they call out
of the blue, they are just a voice on the other end of the line, and you
should be sure to give the client the script that tells them to say,
"I  do not know you and I have never done business with you. I do not
discuss my personal finances over the phone or with strangers." Then get
the collection agency's name and the junk debt buyer's name and hang
up.

9)     Do not "re-contract" with anyone attempting to collect a
debt. Remember that the collection agency, junk debt buyer, and
collection attorney are each trying to re-contract with the alleged
debtor by getting them to admit to the debt; by getting the alleged
debtor (your client) to do their job for them. Their job is to prove you
owe the debt. Innocent communications to a debt collector could be
construed as re-contracting, ultimately holding your client liable for
the debt.

10)   Remember, people who DV (request debt validation) are much less
likely to be sued. People who do not DV are more vulnerable and the
profitable numbers principle applies to them.

11)   Keep good records!!! If your company does not use soft ware,
always, always document your communications. Keep copies of all letters
to and from collection agencies, junk debt buyers and collection
attorneys. When contacting a debt collector, some of them will not take
your dv seriously if it is not sent certified, because they can always
claim they never received it. 

12)   If your client does not have credit card agreements for their
current accounts, advise them to call and get them in case the accounts
were ever to go in arrears. You are less likely to obtain this
information once a credit card account has defaulted, charged-off or is
in collections.

13)   If your client informs you that they are being contacted by a
debt collector via phone, advise them to record the date and time of
debt collector phone calls. Get the name of the company and the
caller's name. Note down the substance of the conversation.
For example, if the collector says there is a summons in the mail to
you, and there is not, that is a threat, which violates the FDCPA.
Keeping this log is a necessary means of recording FDCPA violations. If
one collector continually violates the FDCPA in contacting your client,
their affidavit attesting to your log may be enough for an attorney to
successfully sue on contingency for damages and attorney fees.

14)   Learn about your client's state's debt collection statutes
and consumer protection laws. Unless you already know them, check their
state's statute of limitations (SOL) for all of their outstanding
debts. You should also ask for and check their credit agreements for
which state's laws govern each agreement. It is good to know if that
state's SOL is shorter than your client's state's SOL in case any
legal action is taken against the client. 
Here is a source of state statutes of limitations SOL(s).
https://educreditsolutions.acemlna.com/lt.php?s=da50c2422345422f9a0dc81917526f09&i=429A443A11A2335

15)   Once again...don't confuse the statutes of limitations on
legal actions with the limit on reporting debts by credit reporting
agencies. That is seven years plus 180 days from the first month of
delinquency.

16)   Never, ever give bank account information over the phone.
Remember, 100 percent of debt collectors will lie to you 100 percent of
the time. There is no real urgency that requires you to share that
information, just the lies about court action that the collectors spin
to get you to submit to them.

17)   FYI... if you or your client are still current with your credit
cards and are making payments online by debiting your checking account,
those individually recorded debit-payments are an easy way to document
the contract with the credit card bank. 

18)   In the unlikely event of a court action, always answer a court
summons within the time required stating the appropriate legal defenses
as determined by your local rules of civil procedure. A few pages are
sufficient to avoid an immediate default judgment against the alleged
debtor and to get the collection attorney thinking about the additional
time needed to pursue them in court, as well as to give you and your
client time to prepare their case against the collection attorney.

19)   If the collection agency, junk debt buyer or collection attorney
respond to your DV letter with documentation (usually a few copies of
past account statements and sometimes an unsigned contract) reply with
the follow-up letter (presented in an earlier lesson) disputing the
debt, stating the documentation is insufficient and asking for more.

20)   Remember that debt collectors are not obligated to collect every
debt assigned to them. Their only obligation is to effectively use their
time to collect debts and make money. Most people do not deny, dispute
and request debt validation certified mail return receipt. They are much
easier to collect from than the few that do. 

